      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Deed Classification</div>
        <h2 class="text-widget-title">Statutory Warranty Deed Priority</h2>
        <p class="text-body">
          Standard residential purchases in Washington use a Statutory Warranty Deed, providing buyers with full legal protection against past title defects across the property's entire chain of title.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Contract Review</div>
        <h2 class="text-widget-title">Focus on Schedule B, Section 2</h2>
        <p class="text-body">
          Your preliminary title commitment details specific easements, recorded covenants (CC&amp;Rs), and property restrictions under Schedule B, Section 2 that require careful examination during escrow.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Estate Planning</div>
        <h2 class="text-widget-title">Trusts Cannot Hold Title Directly</h2>
        <p class="text-body">
          In Washington State, a trust agreement is not an independent legal person. Title must be formally vested in the designated trustee on behalf of the trust to establish valid ownership.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Ownership Rights</div>
        <h2 class="text-widget-title">Vesting Dictates Future Rights</h2>
        <p class="text-body">
          How you take title—as a single person, community property, or married as separate property—determines future tax treatment, encumbrance rules, and probate inheritance proceedings.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">1. Deed Types in Washington State Explained</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      A deed is the legal instrument transferring real property ownership from a seller (grantor) to a buyer (grantee). Washington law recognizes four distinct deed formats offering varying levels of title warranty.
    </p>

    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Standard Gold Standard</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">Statutory Warranty Deed</h3>
        <p class="text-body">
          The standard deed used in traditional Northwest MLS transactions. The seller guarantees clear title, promising to defend the buyer against title claims arising throughout the property's entire history.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Limited Warranty</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">Special Warranty Deed</h3>
        <p class="text-body">
          The seller warrants clear title solely during their period of ownership, excluding defects created by prior owners. Standard in corporate relocations, estate liquidations, and bank-owned (REO) sales.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">No Express Covenants</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">Bargain and Sale Deed</h3>
        <p class="text-body">
          Conveys property interest without covenants or warranties against hidden encumbrances. Frequently encountered in tax foreclosure auctions, sheriff sales, and court-ordered conveyances.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Zero Guarantees</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">Quitclaim Deed</h3>
        <p class="text-body">
          Transfers whatever interest the grantor holds with zero warranties or guarantees. Used primarily to resolve title clouds, transfer interest between family members, or process divorce decrees.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">2. Holding Title in Washington State (Vesting Status)</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      Because Washington is one of nine community property states, marital status and vesting language directly control property ownership, conveyance powers, and inheritance pathways.
    </p>

    <div class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <div class="calc-table-wrap">
        <table class="affordability-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" style="width: 32%;">Vesting Classification</th>
              <th scope="col" style="width: 68%;">Legal Parameters &amp; Conveyance Rules</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="calc-stat-label">Single / Unmarried Person</span></td>
              <td class="text-body">Independent individual purchase with complete unilateral conveyance authority</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="background-color: var(--color-surface-gray);">
              <td><span class="calc-stat-label">Community Property</span></td>
              <td class="text-body">Default for married couples; equal shared interest acquired during marriage</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="calc-stat-label">Married as Separate Property</span></td>
              <td class="text-body">Requires non-purchasing spouse to execute a Quitclaim Deed during escrow</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="background-color: var(--color-surface-gray);">
              <td><span class="calc-stat-label">Joint Tenancy with Survivorship</span></td>
              <td class="text-body">Deceased co-owner's share transfers to survivors automatically outside of probate</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="calc-stat-label">Tenancy in Common</span></td>
              <td class="text-body">Default for unmarried co-buyers; fractional shares pass to named heirs via probate</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">3. Washington Trust &amp; Estate Ownership Quirks</h2>
    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Legal Entity Status</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">The Trust Vesting Rule</h3>
        <p class="text-body">
          Under Washington law, a trust agreement is considered a contractual fiduciary relationship rather than an independent legal entity. Title must be vested in the designated trustee on behalf of the trust (e.g., John Doe, Trustee of the Doe Family Revocable Living Trust).
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Probate Authority</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">Estate Purchases &amp; Nonintervention</h3>
        <p class="text-body">
          When purchasing from an estate, title examiners verify that the Personal Representative holds nonintervention powers granted by the probate court, permitting real property conveyances without separate judicial orders.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">4. Reviewing Your Preliminary Title Report</h2>
    <p class="text-body section-body-prose">
      During mutual acceptance, title underwriters generate a Preliminary Commitment. The most vital section for buyer due diligence is <strong>Schedule B, Section 2: Special Exceptions</strong>, which identifies uninsurable parcel-specific encumbrances.
    </p>

    <div class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
      <div class="calc-table-wrap">
        <table class="affordability-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col" style="width: 32%;">Exception Classification</th>
              <th scope="col" style="width: 68%;">Title Impact &amp; Investigation Focus</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="calc-stat-label">Utility &amp; Drainage Easements</span></td>
              <td class="text-body">Recorded municipal or utility rights-of-way crossing portions of the parcel</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="background-color: var(--color-surface-gray);">
              <td><span class="calc-stat-label">CC&amp;Rs</span></td>
              <td class="text-body">HOA covenants governing architectural guidelines, leasing caps, and use limits</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="calc-stat-label">Shared Access &amp; Road Agreements</span></td>
              <td class="text-body">Legally binding maintenance and cost-sharing obligations for joint driveways</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="background-color: var(--color-surface-gray);">
              <td><span class="calc-stat-label">Special Assessments</span></td>
              <td class="text-body">Regional infrastructure charges (e.g., King County Sewer Capacity) tied to parcel</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><span class="calc-stat-label">Monetary Encumbrances</span></td>
              <td class="text-body">Mortgages, tax delinquencies, or mechanic liens requiring full escrow payoff</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section-divider"></div>

  <section class="content-section-centered">
    <h2 class="text-section-title">5. Owner's Policy vs. Lender's Title Insurance</h2>
    <div class="section-grid-static grid-cols-2">
      
      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Equity Protection</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">Owner's Title Insurance Policy</h3>
        <p class="text-body">
          Protects buyer equity up to the full purchase price against hidden liens, forged instruments, or unrecorded title defects. Under standard NWMLS agreements, the seller pays for the Owner's Policy.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="surface-bento calc-strategy-card">
        <div class="text-overline text-accent-alpine">Lender Protection</div>
        <h3 class="text-widget-title">Lender's Title Insurance Policy</h3>
        <p class="text-body">
          Protects the financial institution's mortgage security up to the outstanding loan balance. Paid directly by the borrower as a mandatory closing cost during financing origination.
        </p>
      </article>

    </div>
  </section>

